{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_j7iwe4m6uccx5f3jpyibr2eziqnwjyg2ah5avogyv4q4ias3rojq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification complete. All four phases pass.\n\n## What the library is\n\n`c_src/` is a single 62-line translation unit exporting exactly one function — `int encode_quant(int uni, int step, int pred, int tgt, int tgt2, int lsbit)` — an ADPCM-style quantizer-index refiner that trial-codes three candidate indices (`uni`, `uni±1`) and picks the lowest distortion.\n\n## Phase A — surface mapped\n\n| artifact | content |\n|---|---|\n| `SYMBOLS.md` | 1 API symbol (`encode_quant`) in C; present in Rust. The 4 other `nm -D` entries are gcc/glibc *undefined weak* glue, correctly not mirrored. No C file was left untranslated. |\n| `ERRORS.md` | Grep proves the rejection surface is **empty**: 1 unconditional `return`, zero asserts/`NULL`/`errno`/sentinels/range checks, no pointer args. 20 rows instead enumerate every place a check *could* have been plus every UB-adjacent operation (5 overflow sites, `-INT_MIN`, truncating `/8`, the `d^(d>>31)` pseudo-abs, out-of-range `lsbit` enum values incl. negatives). |\n| `CONFIGS.md` | 9 branch axes → 34 combination rows. |\n\n## Phases B–D — results\n\n- **34/34** Phase B rows and **20/20** Phase C rows pass, plus 3 generic FFI-boundary tests → **57 differential tests, ~2.4M calls/run, 0 divergences**.\n- Symbol diff is **empty**; no unresolved non-libc symbols.\n- `Cargo.toml` has no `[features]` section, so there is exactly **one** combination; `run_all.sh` still enumerates features from the file so it keeps working if any are added.\n- **`src/lib.rs` needed no changes** — the translation was already byte-exact, including the two quirks I confirmed rather than \"fixed\": the second comparison tests `d2 < d0` (not the running best, so `uni2` wins outright when both candidates beat `d0`), and the unused `p3`.\n\n## Two things worth flagging\n\n1. **The suite was initially green against a stale `.so`.** `cargo test` does *not* rebuild a `cdylib`-only lib target — I caught this by mutating `>>5` to `>>4` and seeing all tests still pass. `tests/common/mod.rs` now hard-fails if the `.so` predates `src/`, and `run_all.sh` always builds first.\n2. **Mutation testing, since green tests only mean something if they can fail.** 18 behaviour-changing mutants injected into `src/lib.rs`; **18/18 detected**. A 19th (arithmetic→logical shift inside the `lsbit==4` branch) is provably an *equivalent* mutant — the result is masked with `&1`, so only bits 1–2 of `uni` survive and the shift kind cannot matter. It's documented as such rather than counted as a gap, and I corrected a comment where I had initially claimed the opposite.\n\nBecause the C leans on wrap-around signed arithmetic (formally UB), I also re-ran the suite against `gcc -O1/-O2/-O3/-Ofast` and against the Rust `release` profile (`panic = \"abort\"`): all agree, so the match doesn't depend on the C being unoptimized. `c_src/` sources are unmodified (md5-verified); only the prescribed `c_src/build/` directory was added."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1015,"cache_read_input_tokens":102231,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1015,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3fe78247-6558-4825-9078-0bf15e78a4ec","uuid":"90de4162-802c-4da0-9334-ee90bb6df57b","timestamp":"2026-08-19T11:48:52.101Z"}
